<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#cog-ship">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cog (ship)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The cog was a type of sailing ship used in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> from the 10th to the 14th century, with a flat bottom, high straight sides, a single square sail and, later, a stern rudder and raised castles. Large and cheap to build, it carried bulk cargoes such as grain, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and cloth and became the typical ship of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a> cog, found in 1962, is a well-preserved example from about 1380.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cog-ship.jpg" alt="Cog (ship)" width="320" height="338" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: VollwertBIT · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ubena_von_Bremen_Kiel2007_1_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
